{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788981428,"nonce":"UieKDwZN0f5j6DxBjilph6c6le4=","title":"Sized allocation passes an i64 size, which wasm32 rejects at validation","message":"`hlp_alloc_obj_sized(t, size: usize)` is called from the `New` lowering with\nthe size argument hardcoded to `i64`. `usize` is the target's pointer width,\nso on wasm32 the runtime defines the parameter as `i32` and the module fails\nvalidation at the call:\n\n    type mismatch: expected i32, found i64\n\nAll three wasm conformance suites skip on it -- unit, sys and threads -- so the\npublished table reports wasm as 0/3 with no case data while interp and aot are\n1069/1069.\n\nIntroduced by the sized-allocation change. It is the only functional commit in\nthe window where the skips appear (they are absent one sweep earlier), the\nerror names exactly this shape, and no other native the lowering emits declares\na `usize` parameter as i64 -- `hlp_dyn_seti64`'s i64 is a genuine I64 value, and\nthe AOT data emitter already types its `usize` arguments through\n`TargetAbi::pointer_int_type`.\n\nNot reproducible on a machine without a wasi-sysroot: a stale `ash_runtime.o`\ndoes not define the symbol at all, so the call is an undefined import with no\nsignature to conflict with, and the module builds. CI builds the runtime fresh\nand gets the i32 definition.","files":null}]}